WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.033
- [Instructor] In this video,

00:00:01.033 --> 00:00:05.500
we're going to talk about powers of zero.

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:07.050
And just as a little bit of a reminder,

00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:09.150
let's start with a nonzero number,

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:13.550
just to remind ourselves what
exponentiation is all about.

00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:17.910
So if I were to take
two to the first power,

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:21.090
one way to think about this
is we always start with a one,

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:23.750
and then we multiply this base

00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:25.890
that many times times that one.

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:27.950
So here, we're only gonna have one two,

00:00:27.950 --> 00:00:30.200
so it's gonna be one times two,

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:32.350
which is, of course, equal to two.

00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:35.840
If I were to say what is
two to the second power?

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:40.530
Well, that's going to
be equal to one times,

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:42.740
and now I'm gonna have two twos,

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:47.190
so times two times two,
which is equal to four.

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:49.770
And you could keep going like that.

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:52.030
Now, the reason why I have this one here,

00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:54.710
and we've done this before, is to justify,

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:57.550
and there's many other
good reasons why two

00:00:57.550 --> 00:01:00.330
to the zero power should be equal to one.

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:02.810
But you could see, if we use
the same exact idea here,

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:04.300
you start with a one,

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:07.870
and then you multiply
it by two zero times.

00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:11.320
Well, that's just going
to end up with a one.

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:13.830
So, so far I've told you this
video's about powers of zero,

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:15.470
but I've been doing powers of two.

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:17.870
So let's focus on zero now.

00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:21.830
So what do you think zero to
the first power is going to be?

00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:24.080
Pause this video, and
try to figure that out.

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:26.810
Well, you do the exact same idea.

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:31.700
You start with a one, and then
multiply it by zero one time.

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:33.770
So times zero,

00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:36.200
and this is going to be equal to zero.

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:38.250
What do you think zero to the second power

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:39.860
is going to be equal to?

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:41.760
Pause this video and think about that.

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:46.350
Well, it's going to be
one times zero twice,

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:50.370
so times zero times zero.

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:52.130
And I think you see where this is going.

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:55.200
This is also going to be equal to zero.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:57.550
What do you think zero

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:02.807
to some arbitrary positive integer

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:07.393
is going to be?

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:11.650
Well, it's going to be
equal to one times zero

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:14.320
that positive integer number of times.

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:17.670
So once again, it's going
to be equal to zero.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:19.380
And in general, you can extend that.

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:22.930
Zero to any positive value exponent

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:25.130
is going to give you zero.

00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:27.490
So that's pretty straightforward.

00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:29.870
But there is an
interesting edge case here.

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:33.840
What do you think zero to
the zeroth power should be?

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:35.993
Pause this video and think about that.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:40.150
Well, this is actually contested.

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:42.660
Different people will
tell you different things.

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:46.370
If you use the intuition
behind exponentiation

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:48.270
that we've been using in this video,

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:51.880
you would say, all right,
I would start with a one

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:54.860
and then multiply it by zero zero times.

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:57.360
Or in other words, I just
wouldn't multiply it by zero,

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:00.578
in which case I'm just left with the one,

00:03:00.578 --> 00:03:03.660
the zero to the zeroth power
should be equal to one.

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:05.420
Other folks would say, "Hey, no,

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:07.550
I'm with the zero and
that's the zeroth power,

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:08.800
maybe it should be a zero."

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:11.140
And that's why a lot of
folks leave it undefined.

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:12.410
Most of the time,

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:14.650
you're going to see zero
to the zeroth power,

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:19.650
either being undefined or
that it is equal to one.

